      "pinned": false,
      "postDeleted": false,
      "postID": "5b710f3e41e97a166bb8e6d2",
      "private": false,
      "reactions": null,
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "There are surely several issues with putting a system like this in place, more so without giving users options to control it.  I think any early implementation of a feature like this would be best if done similar to the particle limiter system so as not to cause too many waves while the community gathers more data about how effective it is and ways to do it better. \n\nUnity doesn't provide much data on what happens when two shaders share the same name.  The Editor menus only show one entry even if two shaders exist.   Given that each person has a copy of the shader, I would expect something like a checksum or other unique identifier to handle collision detection/prevention in the game.  That in mind, the internals of how shaders are managed might make it nearly impossible to limit them at all; either too many instances of the shaders to chose from in a UI-based system or the aspect of being able to rename the shader to get around any list setting.   \n\nThe theory is much the same as with the rest of Unity; we don't need access to unlimited everything in order to make something great.  Sure, If I could just use unlimited Unity (bare C#) my world and avatars could do SO much more, but we are limited for a number of very good reasons.  I very much enjoy the sandbox and creative community that VRChat fosters, so I wouldn't want to shoot myself in the feet here either.  \nI simply feel like there is still a better, perhaps unexplored, option than blocking everyone by default or always staying in private rooms to avoid being crashed at random.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
