      "value": "@{5b710f3eb37d4e289398a759|full_name}: The only decent implementation would be to select and block shaders on a per person basis - but there's no point in doing that when we could just block their avatar. \n\nHere's the issue with shaders and how users crash other with them, etc. \n\nThat's all very not detectable. At least not anywhere near consistently. \n\nA whitelist would kill everything that everyone does. \n\nSay they implement a user configurable whitelist. Cool, now you go and add all your favorite shaders that you use.\n\nNow, a friend shows up in the world with a new, cool shader that does something neat, but isn't intrusive. \n\nYou then need to go edit your config to see that. That's not ideal. Nobody wants to take their headset off to go edit a config file constantly. \n\nA blacklist to block any shader that contains a grabpass, which is commonly used to do screen effects, would have to kill EVERY shader using a grabpass. \n\nAnd, well, you now can't have glass-like psuedo refraction on say, a glass part of the avatar. \n\nI could go on for hours about this. \n\nI'm very heavily involved in the shaders community of VRC, the side of it that isn't cancerous. \n\nPersonally, I don't know anyone in that circle who thinks a blacklist/whitelist for shaders would do more good than it would bad.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
