      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "Perhaps a \"Shader Whitelist\" or any client-side mechanics related to it are too much to consider.   \n\nMaybe the better option in the long run would be a specialized kind of CI (Continuous Integration, for the non-developers) that stands to sandbox and test the various components of uploaded content, if the test cases run create a crash or test-case failure condition in this CI environment the uploaded content would just be rejected from use in VRChat. \n\nThis implementation would totally ignore individual users not wanting to see overlay shaders or the like, (since blocking avatars works well enough) however it would at least attempt to cut out content that results in crash conditions or unexpected closing of the client. \nIt would also likely create a bit of delay between uploading content and that content being available for use.   Users with slower/larger uploads might find this more annoying as it could result in more waiting to see if the content is \"approved\" or not. \nThe devs would have their work cut out for them with a system like this, but if a CI / Quality testing system could be automated it might help some with moderating vrchat uploads.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
